{title: The Old Rugged Cross}
{artist: George Bennard}
{key: Bb}
{time: 6/4}
{tempo: 69}

Verse 1
On a [Bb]hill far away stood an [Eb]old rugged cross,
the emblem of [F]suffering and [Bb]shame;
and I love that old cross where the [Eb]dearest and best
for a world of lost [F]sinners was [Bb]slain.

Chorus
So I'll [F]cherish the old rugged [Bb]cross,
till my [Eb]trophies at last I lay [Bb]down;
I will cling to the old rugged [Eb]cross,
and ex[Bb]change it some [F]day for a [Bb]crown.

Verse 2
O that old rugged [Bb]cross, so despised by the world, Has a wondrous [Eb]attraction for me; For the
dear Lamb of God left His [F]glory above To [Bb]bear it
to dark Calvary. So I'll cherish the old rugged cross, Till my trophies at last [Eb]I lay down; I will
cling to the old rugged cross, And exchange it [F]some day for [Bb]a crown.

Verse 3
In that old rugged [Bb]cross, stained with blood so divine, A wondrous beauty [Eb]I see, For 'twas on
that old cross Jesus suffered [F]and died, To [Bb]pardon and
sanctify me. So I'll cherish the old rugged cross, Till my trophies at last [Eb]I lay down; I will
cling to the old rugged cross, And exchange it [F]some day for [Bb]a crown.

Verse 4
To the old rugged [Bb]cross I will ever be true; Its shame and [Eb]reproach gladly bear; Then He'll
call me some day to my [F]home far away, [Bb]Where His
glory forever I'll share. So I'll cherish the old rugged cross, Till my trophies at [Eb]last I lay down; I
will cling to the old rugged cross, And exchange [F]it some day for [Bb]a crown.
